     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><span><div dir=\"ltr\">Hello everyone,<br><br>When we used calculateDiffMeth() without overdispersion correction<br>we got a familiar  volcano plot. (First figure)<br>myDiff_woOD=calculateDiffMeth(meth, mc.cores=2)<br><br>But with OC we got a strange volcano plot. (Second figure)<br>myDiff_wOD=calculateDiffMeth(meth, mc.cores=2, overdispersion = &quot;MN&quot;)<br>Lots of the highly differently methylated CGs became insignificant.<br><br>Is it normal? Are false positives expected among high DMCs?<br>Could you explain me why this is happing? <br><br>Thanks,<br>Ábel<br><br><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"></p><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"></p><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"><a href=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-tRVQRhMh_yY/WTanji5_udI/AAAAAAAAAkA/bSNF00L8ZZwD4o78xxr2WQKStVR1Tm93wCLcB/s1600/woOC.png\" style=\"clear:left;margin-bottom:1em;float:left;margin-right:1em\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-tRVQRhMh_yY/WTanji5_udI/AAAAAAAAAkA/bSNF00L8ZZwD4o78xxr2WQKStVR1Tm93wCLcB/s1600/woOC.png&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793232488904000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2z7OluKXIaLYNAeklv_qcc\"><img src=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-tRVQRhMh_yY/WTanji5_udI/AAAAAAAAAkA/bSNF00L8ZZwD4o78xxr2WQKStVR1Tm93wCLcB/s320/woOC.png\" width=\"320\" height=\"217\" border=\"0\"></a></p><a href=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-KMHCWfUUZgw/WTany1i32hI/AAAAAAAAAkE/RI8fDa_sR6IvLw_l7DZ8nJy9n1LLOLClACLcB/s1600/wOC.png\" style=\"clear:left;marg